  "final": "I fixed the off-by-one-cent totals in `billing/totals.py`. The 6 existing tests pass. I didn't run it against your CSV, so I haven't confirmed that the rows that were off now match.\n\n**Cause:** `line_total_cents` used binary floats and Python's `round()`. Both cause errors:\n- Floats can't represent most decimal prices exactly. A value like `1.005` is stored as 1.00499999…, so it rounds down.\n- `round()` rounds exact ties to the nearest even number, so 12.5 becomes 12. The README says amounts round half up.\n\nEither error can leave a line one cent off. Because the invoice total sums the rounded lines, a bad line also makes the grand total wrong.\n\n**Fix:** the calculation now uses `Decimal` and rounds with `ROUND_HALF_UP`. I spot-checked values that used to round wrongly: `0.125`, `1.005` and `2.675` now give 13, 101 and 268 cents.\n\nI didn't add regression tests. The ties above would be good ones to add to `tests/test_totals.py`.",
